---
title: "Symbolic computation with symcompR"
output: rmarkdown::html_vignette
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{Symbolic computation with symcompR}
  %\VignetteEngine{knitr::rmarkdown}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
---

```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", error = FALSE)
has_sym <- requireNamespace("symcompR", quietly = TRUE)
```

[symcompR](https://github.com/adamrsophie/symcompR) is a computer algebra system for R.
jaxR and symcompR are designed to be loaded together: symcompR manipulates formulas
exactly, jaxR evaluates and differentiates them numerically on arrays. This vignette
shows the three points of contact. symcompR is an optional dependency; the code below
runs only when it is installed.

```{r, eval = has_sym, message = FALSE}
library(symcompR)
library(jaxR)
```

Both packages export `grad`, `hessian` and `jacobian`. Attach jaxR last, as above: its
versions handle functions themselves and pass symbolic expressions on to symcompR, so
one name serves both. (With the other order, write `jaxR::grad` for functions.)

## From a formula to a compiled function

`jax_lambdify(args, expr)` converts a symbolic expression into a jaxR function. The
result is an ordinary jaxR function: it broadcasts over arrays and can be passed to
`jit`, `grad` and `vmap`.

```{r, eval = has_sym}
x <- Symbol("x")
y <- Symbol("y")
expr <- sin(x) * y^2 + exp(x / y)
f <- jax_lambdify(list(x, y), expr)

f(as_jax(c(0.5, 1, 2)), as_jax(c(1, 2, 3)))
jit(f)(as_jax(0.5), as_jax(1))
```

Elementary functions, special functions (`gamma`, `erf`, `digamma`, `zeta`, Bessel
functions, incomplete gamma functions), `Piecewise`, relations, `Min`/`Max` and exact
numbers are translated.

```{r, eval = has_sym}
pw <- jax_lambdify(x, Piecewise(list(x^2, x < 1), list(2 * x, TRUE)))
pw(as_jax(c(0.5, 1, 2)))
```

## Two routes to a derivative

A symbolic derivative and an automatic derivative of the same formula agree:

```{r, eval = has_sym}
dfdx_symbolic <- jax_lambdify(list(x, y), doit(Derivative(expr, x)))
dfdx_auto <- grad(f)                      # with respect to the first argument

dfdx_symbolic(as_jax(0.5), as_jax(2))
dfdx_auto(as_jax(0.5), as_jax(2))
```

The symbolic route gives a formula that you can read and simplify. The automatic route
works for programs that have no closed form (loops, solvers, random numbers) and scales
to millions of parameters.

## One `grad` for both

jaxR's `grad`, `hessian` and `jacobian` dispatch on the first argument: a function is
transformed, a symbolic expression is forwarded to symcompR.

```{r, eval = has_sym}
grad(x^2 * y, list(x, y))                 # symbolic gradient
hessian(x^2 * y, list(x, y))              # symbolic Hessian matrix
grad(function(v) v[0]^2 * v[1])(as_jax(c(3, 2)))    # numerical gradient
```

## Exact constants in array code

Symbolic numbers (integers, rationals, constants such as `pi`) combine with arrays as
weakly typed scalars. Expressions with free symbols are rejected with a clear error.

```{r, eval = has_sym}
as_jax(c(1, 2)) * Rational(1, 2)
```

## A worked example: Newton's method from a formula

```{r, eval = has_sym}
h <- x^3 - 2 * x - 5
hf <- jax_lambdify(x, h)
dhf <- jax_lambdify(x, doit(Derivative(h, x)))
newton <- jit(function(x0) {
  lax_fori_loop(0L, 20L, function(i, z) z - hf(z) / dhf(z), x0)
})
root <- newton(as_jax(2))
root
hf(root)
```
